A||||4||V8Fiero||V6 Cooling problem||||5||
Z||000000||V8Fiero||05-18-2001||07:07 PM||||Im having problem with the cooling system on my new car. If I run with the thermostat (brand new) the car will start to overheat almost immediatley. I can run without the thermostat and the car will take about an hour or so of driving before the temp really starts to get close to too hot. Any suggestions? Radiator clogged maybe?<P>TIA<BR>Scott||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||05-18-2001||09:04 PM||||Now this does seem odd.  My thought is maybe it is not over heating but giving you a false high Reading.  Is your coolant boiling over into reservoir?  Cause with my 86 GT, I had a stuck open t stat and it never produced any significant amount heat even after driving it 2hour on the hwy.<P>Possible air pocket,may have to "burp" cooling system<p>[This message has been edited by PBJ (edited 05-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||V8Fiero||05-18-2001||11:13 PM||||Well, I just got the car and it had been sitting for about a yr or so. On the drive home it started to run hot and into the red. The water temp idiot light came on as well. I shut the car off as soon as I could get off the road. Let it sit for about 5 mins and tried to get home again. Was fine for about 2 miles and started to climb again. I assumed a stuck thermostat so I went the next day to get a new one. Flushed the coolant system out and inserted the new thermostat. Still doing the same thing.<P>Any clue?||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||05-18-2001||11:34 PM||||The coolant temp gauge and light use the same sensor/switch. The ecm uses it own. So check the resistance of the coolant temp gauge, located left hand top of engine. has dark green, and tan wires in connector. (ecm sensor has yellow and black)<BR>Here is what I know about the gauge sensor.<BR>1365 ohms at 100 deg f. (38 deg c.)<BR>55 ohms at 260 deg f. (127 deg c.) <BR>light turns on above 257 deg f. (125 deg c)<P>ok, so what does this mean.  Your engine has not been running for 4 hours (min) you check the resistance of this sensor. TAN wire to ground. If you have 1365 ohms or MORE cuz the outside temp is going to be cooler than 100 deg f. sensor is good. If you find with a cold engine your resistance is less than 1365 ohms replace that sensor.  BE sure you are on a good ground for your meter.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||05-19-2001||12:22 AM||||Or you might want to change out the water pump. If its the original pump you might have the wretched plastic impellor. <P>If the t stat were in, the engine would warm up faster becase thats what its supposed to do. Taking it out allows the heat to disappate through out the system. But if the system is not being circulated none of it matters! <P>But before going into the water pump I would try to rule out all the other possibilities. We had someone come into the shop and ready to plunk down money for a pump and we pulled out a wad of newspaper off of his radiator. Mysterious heating problem was solved! <P>If you put on the radiator fan (turning on the AC) does this affect the temp?<P>Hope yours is just as easy!||||reg||6
